    <item>
      <title>Lecture 1: Introduction to Computational and Systems Biology</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-1-course-introduction-history-of-computational-biology-overview-of-the-course-course-policies-and-mechanics-dna-seque/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-1-course-introduction-history-of-computational-biology-overview-of-the-course-course-policies-and-mechanics-dna-seque/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; In this lecture, Professors Burge, Gifford, and Fraenkel give an historical overview of the field of computational and systems biology, as well as outline the material they plan to cover throughout the semester.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Christopher Burge, David Gifford and Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 10: Markov and Hidden Markov Models of Genomic and Protein Features</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-10-markov-and-hidden-markov-models-of-genomic-and-protein-features/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-10-markov-and-hidden-markov-models-of-genomic-and-protein-features/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Christopher Burge begins by reviewing Lecture 9, then begins his lecture on hidden Markov models (HMM) of genomic and protein features. He addresses the terminology and applications of HMMs, the Viterbi algorithm, and then gives a few examples.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 11: RNA Secondary Structure – Biological Functions and Prediction</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-11-rna-secondary-structure-2013-biological-functions-and-prediction/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-11-rna-secondary-structure-2013-biological-functions-and-prediction/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Lecture 11 is about RNA secondary structure.  Prof. Christopher Burge begins with an introduction and biological examples of RNA structure. He then talks about two approaches for predicting structure: covariation and energy minimization.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 13: Predicting Protein Structure</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-13-predicting-protein-structure/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-13-predicting-protein-structure/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture on predicting protein structure covers refining a partially correct structure. Methods include energy minimization, molecular dynamics, and simulated annealing. He moves on to methods for predicting structure from a sequence of amino acid.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 14: Predicting Protein Interactions</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-14-predicting-protein-interactions/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-14-predicting-protein-interactions/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture is on predicting protein interactions. He discusses structural predictions of protein-protein interactions. He then talks about how measurements of protein-protein interactions are made and Bayes Net prediction of protein-protein interactions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 15: Gene Regulatory Networks</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-15-gene-regulatory-networks/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-15-gene-regulatory-networks/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture by Prof. Ernest Fraenkel is about gene regulatory networks. He begins by finishing Lecture 14&amp;rsquo;s discussion of protein-protein interactions.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 16: Protein Interaction Networks</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-16-protein-interaction-networks/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-16-protein-interaction-networks/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture by Prof. Ernest Fraenkel is on protein interaction networks. He covers network models, including their structure and an analysis. He asks, &amp;ldquo;can we use networks to predict function?&amp;rdquo; He ends with a data integration example.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 17: Logic Modeling of Cell Signaling Networks</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-17-logic-modeling-of-cell-signaling-networks/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-17-logic-modeling-of-cell-signaling-networks/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Doug Lauffenburger delivers a guest lecture on the topic of logic modeling of cell signaling networks. He begins by giving a conceptual background of the subject, and then discusses an example involving hepatocyes (liver cells).&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Doug Lauffenburger&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 18: Analysis of Chromatin Structure</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-18-analysis-of-chromatin-structure/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-18-analysis-of-chromatin-structure/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture begins with the epigenetic state, which regulates gene function without changing DNA. Then, how to estimate the protein occupancy of the genome via computational methods. Lastly, how to map enhancers to their regulatory targets.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 19: Discovering Quantitative Trait Loci (QTLs)</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-19-discovering-quantitative-trait-loci-qtls/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-19-discovering-quantitative-trait-loci-qtls/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture is guided by the question &amp;ldquo;Where is missing heritability found?&amp;rdquo; Prof. David Gifford discusses computational models that can predict phenotype from genotype. He then discusses how to discover and model quantitative trait loci.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 2: Local Alignment (BLAST) and Statistics</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-2-local-alignment-blast-and-statistics/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-2-local-alignment-blast-and-statistics/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; In this lecture, Professor Burge reviews classical and next-generation sequencing. He then introduces local alignment (BLAST) and some of the associated statistics.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 20: Human Genetics, SNPs, and Genome Wide Associate Studies</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-20-human-genetics-snps-and-genome-wide-associate-studies/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-20-human-genetics-snps-and-genome-wide-associate-studies/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture by Prof. David Gifford is on human genetics. He covers how scientists discover variation in the human genome. He discusses how to prioritize variants based on their importance. And then covers how to prove causation, not just correlation.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 21: Synthetic Biology: From Parts to Modules to Therapeutic Systems</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-21-synthetic-biology-from-parts-to-modules-to-therapeutic-systems/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-21-synthetic-biology-from-parts-to-modules-to-therapeutic-systems/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This guest lecture by Prof. Ron Weiss is on synthetic biology. Prof. Weiss describes how he came to be a synthetic biologist, followed by an overview of the field. He covers basic , technologies for scalability, and programmable therapeutics.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ron Weiss&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 22: Causality, Natural Computing, and Engineering Genomes</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-22-causality-natural-computing-and-engineering-genomes/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-22-causality-natural-computing-and-engineering-genomes/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This guest lecture by Prof. George Church is on the topic of causality, in particular, how to use genomic data and the tools of natural computing to differentiate between correlation and causation.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. George Church&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 3: Global Alignment of Protein Sequences (NW, SW, PAM, BLOSUM)</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-3-global-alignment-of-protein-sequences-nw-sw-pam-blosum/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-3-global-alignment-of-protein-sequences-nw-sw-pam-blosum/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; In this lecture, Prof. Burge discusses global sequence alignment and gapped local sequence alignment. He later talks about substitution matrices for protein comparison.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 4: Comparative Genomic Analysis of Gene Regulation</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-4-comparative-genomic-analysis-of-gene-regulation/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-4-comparative-genomic-analysis-of-gene-regulation/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Burge discusses comparative genomics. He begins with a review of global alignment of protein sequences, then talks about Markov models, the Jukes-Cantor model, and Kimura models. He discusses types of selection: natural, negative, and positive.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 5: Library Complexity and Short Read Alignment (Mapping)</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-5-library-complexity-and-short-read-alignment-mapping/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-5-library-complexity-and-short-read-alignment-mapping/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Gifford talks about library complexity as it relates to genome sequencing. He explains how to create a full-text minute-size (FM) index, which involves a Burrows-Wheeler transform (BWT). He ends with how to deal with the problem of mismatching.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 6: Genome Assembly</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-6-genome-assembly/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-6-genome-assembly/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Gifford talks about two different ways to assemble a genome de novo. The first approach is overlap layout consensus assemblers, as exemplified by string graph assemblers. The second approach is de Bruijn graph-based assemblers.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 8: RNA-sequence Analysis: Expression, Isoforms</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-8-rna-sequence-analysis-expression-isoforms/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-8-rna-sequence-analysis-expression-isoforms/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture by Prof. David Gifford is about RNA-seq (RNA sequencing), a method of characterizing RNA molecules through next-generation sequencing. He begins with the principles of RNA-seq, and then moves on to how to analyze the data generated by RNA-seq.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Lecture 9: Modeling and Discovery of Sequence Motifs</title>
      <link>/resources/lecture-9-modeling-and-discovery-of-sequence-motifs-gibbs-sampler-alternatives/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/lecture-9-modeling-and-discovery-of-sequence-motifs-gibbs-sampler-alternatives/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; This lecture by Prof. Christopher Burge covers modeling and discovery of sequence motifs. He gives the example of the Gibbs sampling algorithm. He covers information content of a motif, and he ends with parameter estimation for motif models.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Christopher Burge&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Leture 12: Introduction to Protein Structure; Structure Comparison and Classification</title>
      <link>/resources/leture-12-introduction-to-protein-structure-structure-comparison-and-classification/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/leture-12-introduction-to-protein-structure-structure-comparison-and-classification/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; Professor Ernest Fraenkel begins his unit of the course, which moves across scales, from atoms to proteins to networks. This lecture is about the structure of proteins, and how biological phenomena make sense in light of protein structure.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. Ernest Fraenkel&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Leture 7: ChIP-seq Analysis; DNA-protein Interactions</title>
      <link>/resources/leture-7-chip-seq-analysis-dna-protein-interactions/data.json</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/resources/leture-7-chip-seq-analysis-dna-protein-interactions/data.json</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Description:&lt;/strong&gt; In this lecture, Prof. David Gifford discusses transcriptional regulation. He talks about techniques that can elucidate how genes are regulated, and how gene regulators interact with the genome.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Instructor:&lt;/strong&gt; Prof. David Gifford&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
